<h2>The mirror-image litigation</h2>
<p>The dispute concerns the word mark “SUPREME”. Supremo India Private Limited moved first in Indore: its suit, CS (Comm) No. 134 of 2025 before the District Judge (Commercial) Court, sought a permanent injunction against Supreme Industries’ use of the mark, disclosure of assets, takedown of goods from e-commerce platforms, and Rs 15,00,000 in damages. Supreme Industries answered with its own suit in the Delhi High Court — CS (Comm) No. 1294 of 2025 — alleging infringement of its registered trademark, passing off, copyright infringement in its label and logo, and dilution, with claims of Rs 2,00,01,000 in damages and a prayer to declare “SUPREME” a well-known mark.</p>
<p>Each party then petitioned the Supreme Court to move the other’s suit: Supreme Industries sought transfer of the Indore suit to Delhi, and Supremo India sought transfer of the Delhi suit to Indore.</p>

<h2>What the Court did</h2>
<p>The Bench of Justice S.V.N. Bhatti and Justice N.V. Anjaria recorded two things that decided the matter. First, counsel on both sides accepted that the two suits should be tried by a single court — the sensible position, since both suits will turn on the same rival claims to the same mark. Second, neither side would concede the other’s home forum. The Court resolved the deadlock by choosing neither: both suits now stand transferred to the City Civil Court, Ahmedabad.</p>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep">Indore suit CS (Comm) 134/2025 — transferred from the District Judge (Commercial) Court, Indore to the City Civil Court, Ahmedabad.</div>
<div class="fstep">Delhi suit CS (Comm) 1294/2025 — transferred from the Delhi High Court to the City Civil Court, Ahmedabad.</div>
<div class="fstep">Both courts of origin to transmit the plaints and records promptly.</div>
<div class="fstep">Parties to appear before the City Civil Court, Ahmedabad on 26 November 2026.</div>
</div>
<p>The order is expressly not a judgment on the merits: the Court recorded that it did not express a view on the rival claims and left several arguments unaddressed.</p>

<h2>Why consolidation matters in trademark fights</h2>
<p>Parallel suits over one mark create three concrete risks: inconsistent interim orders — one court restraining use while the other declines; duplicated evidence, with the same witnesses and sales records proved twice; and a race to judgment in which each decree is then wielded against the other proceeding. A single forum eliminates all three. The price is convenience — here, both sides must now litigate in a city that is home to neither suit, which is precisely what made the choice workable as a neutral solution.</p>
<div class="note">
<p>Practice point: in cross-suit situations, a transfer petition under Section 25 CPC is often the most consequential interlocutory step in the litigation. Parties who cannot agree on a forum should expect the Supreme Court to weigh neutrality, and should assess early whether consolidation helps or hurts their evidentiary position — because once the suits sit before one judge, the pleadings in each become ammunition in both.</p>
</div>

<h2>The takeaway</h2>
<p>For businesses in brand disputes, the decision illustrates a practical reality: where both sides have filed, the venue question will likely be answered by consolidation, and not necessarily in either side’s preferred city. Building the record for — or against — a particular forum, and moving promptly on transfer, is as much a part of trademark strategy as the merits themselves.</p>
